    "body": ">> For no-cors requests, add a Sec-Accept-Content-Readability: public > request header to advertise support to the origin for marking responses as readable.\n\n> Is this part necessary?\n\nMaybe not if we caught it in time with recent additions to the web platform tests for compression dictionary.\n\nThe risk is if there is client that sends \"Accept-Encoding: dcb\" for `no-cors` requests that the response will work for clients that support readibility override but fail for clients that don't with no way to distinguish them.\n\nThe WPTs were updated to make sure the Accept-Encoding isn't sent for no-cors requests. Chrome hasn't sent it since the feature launched and is the only browser that has shipped support so it should be safe to unilaterally assume that any client that includes the encoding does it because it also supports readibility override.\n\nAs far as I know, compression dictionary encoding is the only feature that depends on it where the origin needs to know and varies the response as a result.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/fetch/issues/comments/3021633491/reactions",
