    "created_at": "2025-11-18T15:39:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-11-18T15:39:16Z",
    "body": "Sorry, I was thinking more along the lines of \"this response is always safe and readable from anywhere\" like the issue started with (public, static resources).\n\nIf a response contains sensitive user data that the origin explicitly marks as `CORP: cross-origin` for a context where it thinks it is in a \"safe\" cross-origin then I can see why the context is needed across redirects. That feels like a generally risky thing to do for opaque no-cors requests but makes sense to protect against.",
